{"rows":[{"n":1,"file":"topics/01-java-core-jvm.json","topic_type":"core"},{"n":2,"file":"topics/02-java-8-25-java-vs-go.json","topic_type":"core"},{"n":3,"file":"topics/03-spring-boot-deep-build.json","topic_type":"core"},{"n":4,"file":"topics/04-rest-grpc-webflux.json","topic_type":"design"},{"n":5,"file":"topics/05-db-core-index-lock.json","topic_type":"data"},{"n":6,"file":"topics/06-db-scaling.json","topic_type":"data"},{"n":7,"file":"topics/07-sql-nosql-db-engines.json","topic_type":"data"},{"n":8,"file":"topics/08-message-queue.json","topic_type":"data"},{"n":9,"file":"topics/09-distributed-tx-fintech.json","topic_type":"design"},{"n":12,"file":"topics/12-architecture-patterns.json","topic_type":"design"},{"n":13,"file":"topics/13-security-oauth2.json","topic_type":"platform"},{"n":14,"file":"topics/14-devops-k8s-best-practices.json","topic_type":"platform"},{"n":15,"file":"topics/15-network-i-o-models.json","topic_type":"core"},{"n":16,"file":"topics/16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.json","topic_type":"algorithm","system_design_items":["16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.presenting-dissecting-the-ota-project.q1","16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.presenting-dissecting-the-ota-project.q2","16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.presenting-dissecting-the-ota-project.q3","16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.presenting-dissecting-the-ota-project.q4","16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.presenting-dissecting-the-ota-project.q5","16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.whiteboard-dsa.q1"]},{"n":17,"file":"topics/17-rest-api-design.json","topic_type":"design"},{"n":18,"file":"topics/18-query-optimization.json","topic_type":"data"},{"n":19,"file":"topics/19-dsa-leetcode.json","topic_type":"algorithm"},{"n":20,"file":"topics/20-observability-sre.json","topic_type":"platform"},{"n":21,"file":"topics/21-linux-production-debug.json","topic_type":"platform"},{"n":22,"file":"topics/22-low-level-design-ood.json","topic_type":"algorithm"},{"n":23,"file":"topics/23-java-concurrency-coding.json","topic_type":"algorithm"},{"n":24,"file":"topics/24-domain-driven-design.json","topic_type":"design"},{"n":25,"topic_type":"microservice","file":"topics/25-microservice.json"},{"n":26,"file":"topics/26-testing-strategy.json","topic_type":"core"},{"n":28,"file":"topics/28-distributed-lock-lease.json","topic_type":"design"}],"row":{"n":24,"file":"topics/24-domain-driven-design.json","topic_type":"design"},"meta":{"topic_type":"design","key":"24-domain-driven-design","vi":{"label":"Domain-Driven Design","title":"Domain-Driven Design — chia miền và mô hình hoá","intro":"Chủ đề 12 nêu DDD trong bốn mục; đây là phần bị đào sâu khi bạn nói 'em làm theo DDD'. Ranh giới context tìm bằng cách nào, aggregate to nhỏ ra sao, domain event khác integration event chỗ nào — và quan trọng nhất: khi nào KHÔNG nên dùng.","tags":["Bounded context","Ubiquitous language","Aggregate","Domain event","Context mapping","Event storming","ACL"]},"en":{"label":"Domain-Driven Design","title":"Domain-Driven Design — splitting the domain and modelling it","intro":"Topic 12 introduces DDD in four items; this is what gets dug into once you say you follow DDD. How to find context boundaries, how big an aggregate should be, how a domain event differs from an integration event — and most importantly, when not to use any of it.","tags":["Bounded context","Ubiquitous language","Aggregate","Domain event","Context mapping","Event storming","ACL"]},"created_at":"2026-07-31","updated_at":"2026-08-23"},"topicMeta":{"1":{"en":{"label":"Java Core & JVM"},"vi":{"label":"Java Core & JVM"}},"2":{"en":{"label":"Java 8→25 & Java vs Go"},"vi":{"label":"Java 8→25 & Java vs Go"}},"3":{"en":{"label":"Spring Boot deep dive + build"},"vi":{"label":"Spring Boot deep + build"}},"4":{"en":{"label":"REST/gRPC & WebFlux"},"vi":{"label":"REST/gRPC & WebFlux"}},"5":{"en":{"label":"DB core: indexes & locking"},"vi":{"label":"DB core: index & lock"}},"6":{"en":{"label":"DB scaling"},"vi":{"label":"DB scaling"}},"7":{"en":{"label":"SQL/NoSQL & DB engines"},"vi":{"label":"SQL/NoSQL & DB engines"}},"8":{"en":{"label":"Message queue"},"vi":{"label":"Message queue"}},"9":{"en":{"label":"Distributed tx (fintech)"},"vi":{"label":"Distributed tx (fintech)"}},"10":{"en":{"label":"System design + rate limit"},"vi":{"label":"System design + rate limit"}},"11":{"en":{"label":"System design cases"},"vi":{"label":"System design cases"}},"12":{"en":{"label":"Architecture & patterns"},"vi":{"label":"Architecture & patterns"}},"13":{"en":{"label":"Security & OAuth2"},"vi":{"label":"Security & OAuth2"}},"14":{"en":{"label":"DevOps · K8s · best practices"},"vi":{"label":"DevOps · K8s · best practices"}},"15":{"en":{"label":"Network & I/O models"},"vi":{"label":"Network & I/O models"}},"16":{"en":{"label":"Project · concurrency · whiteboard"},"vi":{"label":"Project · concurrency · whiteboard"}},"17":{"en":{"label":"REST API design"},"vi":{"label":"Thiết kế REST API"}},"18":{"en":{"label":"Query optimisation"},"vi":{"label":"Tối ưu query"}},"19":{"en":{"label":"DSA & LeetCode"},"vi":{"label":"DSA & LeetCode"}},"20":{"en":{"label":"Observability & SRE"},"vi":{"label":"Observability & SRE"}},"21":{"en":{"label":"Linux & production debug"},"vi":{"label":"Linux & production debug"}},"22":{"en":{"label":"Low-level design / OOD"},"vi":{"label":"Low-level design / OOD"}},"23":{"en":{"label":"Java concurrency coding"},"vi":{"label":"Java concurrency coding"}},"24":{"en":{"label":"Domain-Driven Design"},"vi":{"label":"Domain-Driven Design"}},"25":{"en":{"label":"Microservices at Scale — Technical Mastery Track"},"vi":{"label":"Microservices at Scale — Technical Mastery Track"}},"26":{"en":{"label":"Testing"},"vi":{"label":"Testing"}},"27":{"en":{"label":"API Gateway & Identity Edge"},"vi":{"label":"API Gateway & Identity Edge"}},"28":{"en":{"label":"Distributed Lock & Lease"},"vi":{"label":"Distributed Lock & Lease"}}},"reviewedAt":{},"lang":"en","body":{"sections":[{"title":"Strategic — splitting the domain","items":[{"id":"24-domain-driven-design.strategic-splitting-the-domain.q1","q":"What problem does DDD actually solve? What does ubiquitous language really mean?","a":"DDD [[r:is not]] a catalog of patterns to copy. It solves a [[o:communication]] problem: the business uses one vocabulary, the code uses another, and every translation between them is another opportunity for bugs.\n\n**Ubiquitous language** = *one* shared vocabulary used by developers, BAs, QA, and domain experts — and [[g:represented verbatim in the code]].\n\n<pre><code><span class=\"c\">// The business says: \"hold a seat\", \"issue a ticket\", \"refund a ticket\"</span>\n<span class=\"c\">//</span>\n<span class=\"c\">// WRONG — technical vocabulary that must be translated at every meeting</span>\nbookingService.<span class=\"f\">updateStatus</span>(id, <span class=\"n\">2</span>);\nbookingRepository.<span class=\"f\">setFlag</span>(id, <span class=\"k\">true</span>);\n<span class=\"c\">//</span>\n<span class=\"c\">// RIGHT — reads exactly like a sentence the business uses</span>\nbooking.<span class=\"f\">holdSeat</span>(seatNo);\nbooking.<span class=\"f\">issueTicket</span>(gdsRef);\nbooking.<span class=\"f\">refund</span>(reason);</code></pre>\n\nA simple test: [[o:read a method aloud to a domain expert]]. If they understand it immediately → the language is shared. If you have to explain that \"status 2 means the seat is held\" → it is not shared yet.\n\n**But the language is consistent only within a [[b:bounded context]]** — not across the entire company. That is the subject of 24.2.\n\n:::warn The biggest trap\nDDD [[r:is not]] \"having a `domain` directory\" or \"using `@Entity`\". Many projects claim to practice DDD but are really layered architecture with renamed directories: entities have only getters and setters, while every rule lives in a `Service` (12.11 anemic domain). Directory structure is not DDD; modeling is.\n:::\n\n:::deep\n- DDD has a cost: it requires [[g:a domain expert working closely with the team]]. Without one, everything else is ceremony — this is a prerequisite that books are often skimmed past.\n- Separate its two parts clearly: [[o:strategic design]] (dividing contexts and drawing maps — the highest-value part, which everyone should know) and [[o:tactical design]] (aggregates, VOs, and repositories — worth the cost only in the core domain).\n- Classify subdomains to decide where to invest: [[g:core]] (competitive advantage — engineer it carefully and apply DDD here), [[g:supporting]] (necessary but not differentiating — keep it simple), and [[g:generic]] (sending email, generating PDFs — buy it or use a library).\n- Being able to articulate all three is a sign of genuine understanding.\n- The shared language must [[r:live in the code]]: when a business term changes, rename the class as well. A glossary in Confluence while the code still says `status = 2` is a dead language.\n- When NOT to use it (12-architecture-patterns.domain-driven-design.q3): plain CRUD, a thin domain, a short-lived project, or a team with no DDD experience. Explaining when it is inappropriate matters as much as explaining when it is appropriate.\n:::","difficulty":"core"},{"id":"24-domain-driven-design.strategic-splitting-the-domain.q2","q":"Bounded contexts: how do you identify the right boundaries?","a":"A [[b:bounded context]] is the scope within which one model and one vocabulary have consistent meaning. Outside that boundary, the same term may mean something different.\n\n<figure class='dgm'><svg viewBox='0 0 680 290' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' font-family='IBM Plex Mono, monospace' font-size='10' text-anchor='middle'>\n<defs><marker id='dd1' markerWidth='9' markerHeight='9' refX='6' refY='3' orient='auto'><path d='M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 z' fill='#42536C'/></marker></defs>\n<text x='340' y='18' fill='#14233A' font-weight='600' font-size='11.5'>The same word \"Ticket\" — three contexts, three different meanings</text>\n<rect x='24' y='36' width='190' height='96' rx='9' fill='#E4F1EB' stroke='#0B6E4F' stroke-width='1.5'/>\n<text x='119' y='56' fill='#085239' font-weight='600'>BOOKING</text>\n<text x='119' y='76' fill='#42536C'>Ticket = held seat</text><text x='119' y='91' fill='#42536C'>+ passenger + hold expiry</text><text x='119' y='110' fill='#7E8DA3'>concern: is a seat available?</text>\n<rect x='245' y='36' width='190' height='96' rx='9' fill='#E7EAF6' stroke='#3D4E8F' stroke-width='1.5'/>\n<text x='340' y='56' fill='#3D4E8F' font-weight='600'>PAYMENT</text>\n<text x='340' y='76' fill='#42536C'>Ticket = amount to collect</text><text x='340' y='91' fill='#42536C'>+ currency + status</text><text x='340' y='110' fill='#7E8DA3'>concern: has it been paid in full?</text>\n<rect x='466' y='36' width='190' height='96' rx='9' fill='#faf5e9' stroke='#9A7A41' stroke-width='1.5'/>\n<text x='561' y='56' fill='#9A7A41' font-weight='600'>TICKETING</text>\n<text x='561' y='76' fill='#42536C'>Ticket = issued e-ticket</text><text x='561' y='91' fill='#42536C'>+ GDS reference + itinerary</text><text x='561' y='110' fill='#7E8DA3'>concern: has it been issued?</text>\n<g stroke='#42536C' stroke-width='1.4' marker-end='url(#dd1)'><line x1='214' y1='84' x2='241' y2='84'/><line x1='435' y1='84' x2='462' y2='84'/></g>\n<text x='227' y='72' fill='#7E8DA3' font-size='9'>event</text><text x='448' y='72' fill='#7E8DA3' font-size='9'>event</text>\n<rect x='24' y='152' width='632' height='36' rx='7' fill='#FBFCFE' stroke='#DBE1EA'/>\n<text x='340' y='168' fill='#14233A' font-weight='600'>The right boundary is where LANGUAGE changes meaning, not where DB tables split</text>\n<text x='340' y='182' fill='#42536C'>One Ticket class for all three = a god object; every change breaks something else</text>\n<text x='340' y='212' fill='#AE4536' font-weight='600'>Signs of the WRONG boundary</text>\n<text x='340' y='230' fill='#42536C'>· one business change always requires changes in ≥ 2 contexts</text>\n<text x='340' y='246' fill='#42536C'>· one context must query another context's table to operate</text>\n<text x='340' y='262' fill='#42536C'>· the same term always prompts \"which Ticket do you mean?\"</text>\n<text x='340' y='280' fill='#7E8DA3'>With the right boundaries, each context answers its own questions</text>\n</svg></figure>\n\n**How to find boundaries — in descending order of reliability:**\n\n1. [[g:The language changes meaning]] — the strongest signal. If you must ask *\"which Ticket do you mean?\"*, you are standing at a boundary.\n2. [[g:Different rates of change]] — a part that changes weekly should not be coupled to one that changes once a year.\n3. [[g:Ownership]] — under Conway's law, system boundaries tend to align with team boundaries. Going against that means fighting the organization.\n4. [[g:Invariants that must be maintained together]] — do [[r:not]] separate things that require immediate consistency (24-domain-driven-design.tactical-modelling-it.q2).\n\n**A boundary is [[r:not]]:** a database table, a technical module (`web`, `service`), or a JPA entity. Splitting by table is a reliable way to create a distributed monolith (12-architecture-patterns.patterns-principles.q3).\n\n:::deep\n- A bounded context [[o:may]] contain several aggregates; an aggregate [[r:must not]] span two contexts.\n- In source code, a context should be a [[g:top-level module/package]] (12-architecture-patterns.clean-layered-building-it-for-real.q8). Spring Modulith can enforce that boundary inside a monolith without extracting a service.\n- Boundaries are [[o:not fixed]]: redraw them as understanding grows. Redrawing one in a monolith is a refactor; redrawing one after extracting microservices is a project. That is precisely the case for \"modular monolith first\" (12-architecture-patterns.patterns-principles.q5).\n- A common mistake is creating a `Common`/`Shared` context for shared entities. It becomes a shared dependency that tightly couples the contexts; a change by either team can break the other; prefer [[g:controlled duplication]] within each context.\n- For a legacy system that cannot be separated, place an [[o:Anti-Corruption Layer]] (12-architecture-patterns.patterns-principles.q6) at the boundary so the old model does not leak into the new context.\n- A probing question: *\"Are a context and a microservice the same thing?\"* — [[r:no]]. A context is a design concept; a service is a deployment unit. A service should contain exactly one context, but a context can live perfectly well inside a monolith.\n:::","difficulty":"advanced"},{"id":"24-domain-driven-design.strategic-splitting-the-domain.q3","q":"Context mapping: what relationships can exist between two bounded contexts?","a":"After contexts are separated, they still have to communicate. A [[b:context map]] describes the *power relationships* between them — a subject that few people mention but that often appears in senior interviews.\n\n<table class=\"cmp\"><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Use when</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><b>Partnership</b></td><td>two teams succeed or fail together and collaborate closely</td><td>the two contexts must release together</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Shared Kernel</b></td><td>share part of the model/code</td><td>rare — both sides must agree on every change</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Customer / Supplier</b></td><td>the upstream serves the downstream, with negotiation</td><td>both are in one company and the downstream has influence</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Conformist</b></td><td>the downstream <b>accepts</b> the upstream model</td><td>there is no negotiating leverage (a third-party API)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Anti-Corruption Layer</b></td><td>the downstream translates into its own model</td><td>the upstream model is polluted or legacy</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Open Host Service</b></td><td>the upstream exposes a common API to many consumers</td><td>several downstreams use it</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Published Language</b></td><td>a shared, versioned contract</td><td>OpenAPI/Protobuf/event schema</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Separate Ways</b></td><td>do not integrate; accept duplication</td><td>integration cost &gt; benefit</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nIn the OTA example, `Booking` ↔ `Payment` is **Customer/Supplier**. `Ticketing` ↔ GDS (Amadeus/Sabre) uses an **ACL**: the GDS model is very old and cannot be changed, so translation belongs at the boundary. A public partner API is **Open Host + Published Language** (17-rest-api-design.contracts-errors-lifecycle.q4).\n\n:::deep\n- [[o:The real value of a context map]] is not its boxes and arrows, but how it exposes *organizational relationships*: \"the other team will never change it for us\" → Conformist or ACL, not a fantasy Partnership.\n- [[g:An ACL pays off most]] when integrating with a legacy system or third party: a thin translation layer prevents their polluted model from spreading into your domain. The cost is mapping; the benefits are a clean domain and a single place to change when replacing the provider.\n- [[r:A Shared Kernel is debt]]: it sounds economical, but forces two teams to coordinate every change. Use it only when one team genuinely owns both contexts.\n- Integration through [[g:events]] (24-domain-driven-design.tactical-modelling-it.q3) is often healthier than synchronous calls: a failing downstream cannot take down the upstream, and the relationship naturally becomes Open Host + Published Language.\n- Separate Ways is underrated: letting two contexts calculate tax independently can be far cheaper than building a shared `TaxService` that makes everyone wait.\n- Draw the context map [[o:before]] extracting microservices. Without one, extraction merely splits the system by database table — the recipe for a distributed monolith.\n:::","difficulty":"advanced"},{"id":"24-domain-driven-design.strategic-splitting-the-domain.q4","q":"Event storming: how do you run a workshop that reveals the boundaries?","a":"Event storming is the most practical way to move from *\"nobody knows what the system does\"* to a *context map* in a single workshop. Developers, BAs, and domain experts stand together at one wall.\n\n**Sticky-note color conventions (standardized; do not invent your own):**\n\n<table class=\"cmp\"><thead><tr><th>Color</th><th>Represents</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Orange</td><td><b>Domain event</b> — something that happened, in the past tense</td><td><code>SeatHeld</code>, <code>PaymentCaptured</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Blue</td><td><b>Command</b> — an intention, in the imperative mood</td><td><code>HoldSeat</code>, <code>IssueTicket</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Yellow</td><td><b>Actor</b> — who initiates it</td><td>Customer, customer-service agent</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Pink</td><td><b>External system</b></td><td>GDS, PSP</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Purple</td><td><b>Policy</b> — \"whenever X, then Y\"</td><td>seat hold expires → release seat</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Pale yellow</td><td><b>Aggregate</b> — where the command lands</td><td>Booking, Payment</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Red</td><td><b>Hot spot</b> — a disputed or unclear point</td><td>\"how is a partial cancellation charged?\"</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n**Four steps:**\n\n1. Put [[o:all]] domain events on the wall without ordering them yet — whenever someone names one, add it.\n2. Arrange them on a [[o:timeline]] from left → right. Contradictions surface immediately; mark them with red notes.\n3. Add the command, actor, and external system before each event; add policies between events.\n4. Cluster events that tend to occur together → [[g:each cluster is an aggregate]]; where the language changes meaning → [[g:a context boundary]].\n\n:::deep\n- [[o:Red]] notes are the most valuable output of the workshop. They pinpoint where the business has not reached agreement — exactly where bugs appear when developers guess.\n- The golden rule: [[r:do not discuss technical solutions]] during the workshop. Someone saying \"we'll put this on Kafka\" pulls the entire room off course.\n- Events must be in the [[g:past tense]] (`SeatHeld`, not `HoldSeat`). That sounds minor, but it forces everyone to discuss *what happened* rather than *how to implement it*.\n- There are three scopes: [[o:big picture]] (the whole company, to find contexts), [[o:process modeling]] (one process), and [[o:design level]] (deriving aggregates and commands directly). Choose the scope that fits the question you need to answer.\n- Before everyone leaves, turn the result into a [[g:context map]] (24-domain-driven-design.strategic-splitting-the-domain.q3) plus an aggregate list (24-domain-driven-design.tactical-modelling-it.q2). Otherwise, the next day all that remains is a photograph nobody reads.\n- Remote sessions work in Miro or FigJam, but a physical wall is still better because people can stand up, point, and argue more naturally.\n:::","difficulty":"extra"}]},{"title":"Tactical — modelling it","items":[{"id":"24-domain-driven-design.tactical-modelling-it.q1","q":"Entity vs. Value Object: what distinguishes them, and why are Value Objects more useful than they seem?","a":"<table class=\"cmp\"><thead><tr><th></th><th>Entity</th><th>Value Object</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Identity</td><td>has an <b>id</b>; distinguished by identity</td><td>has no id; equal when <b>all fields are equal</b></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Mutable?</td><td>yes; its state changes over time</td><td><b>immutable</b> — replace it to make a change</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Example</td><td><code>Booking</code>, <code>Passenger</code></td><td><code>Money</code>, <code>SeatNumber</code>, <code>DateRange</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Equality</td><td><code>equals</code> by id</td><td><code>equals</code> by value</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n**Value Objects are among the most underused and highest-leverage building blocks.** They turn runtime mistakes into compile-time errors:\n\n<pre><code><span class=\"c\">// Before: everything is a primitive — \"primitive obsession\"</span>\n<span class=\"k\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">refund</span>(String bookingId, <span class=\"k\">long</span> amount, String currency) { ... }\n<span class=\"f\">refund</span>(seatNo, price, code);          <span class=\"c\">// compiles successfully; completely wrong</span>\n<span class=\"c\">//</span>\n<span class=\"c\">// After: types express intent</span>\n<span class=\"k\">public record</span> Money(BigDecimal amount, Currency currency) {\n    <span class=\"k\">public</span> <span class=\"f\">Money</span> {\n        <span class=\"k\">if</span> (amount.<span class=\"f\">scale</span>() &gt; currency.<span class=\"f\">getDefaultFractionDigits</span>())\n            <span class=\"k\">throw new</span> IllegalArgumentException(<span class=\"s\">\"invalid decimal scale for \"</span> + currency);\n    }\n    <span class=\"k\">public</span> Money <span class=\"f\">plus</span>(Money o) {\n        <span class=\"k\">if</span> (!currency.<span class=\"f\">equals</span>(o.currency)) <span class=\"k\">throw new</span> DomainException(<span class=\"s\">\"currency mismatch\"</span>);\n        <span class=\"k\">return new</span> Money(amount.<span class=\"f\">add</span>(o.amount), currency);\n    }\n}\n<span class=\"k\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">refund</span>(BookingId id, Money amount) { ... }   <span class=\"c\">// an invalid call does not compile</span></code></pre>\n\nAdding amounts in two different currencies is now [[g:impossible]], rather than something developers must remember not to do.\n\n:::deep\n- Java 16+ `record` types are an almost perfect fit for VOs: they are immutable, implement `equals`/`hashCode` with value semantics, and provide a compact constructor for validation. Before records, all of that required hand-written code — one reason VOs are less common in older Java codebases.\n- VOs are the [[g:most natural place]] for small rules: `Money.plus` rejects different currencies, while `DateRange` rejects an end date before its start date. Put a rule here and callers cannot forget to apply it.\n- Immutability gives [[o:thread safety]] for free (23-java-concurrency-coding.classic-synchronisation-problems.q4): a VO can be shared between threads without locking.\n- Persist VOs in JPA with `@Embeddable`/`@Embedded`, or an `AttributeConverter` for a single-field VO. If the domain model is separate from persistence entities (12-architecture-patterns.clean-layered-building-it-for-real.q2), this presents no difficulty at all.\n- [[r:Do not]] turn every String into a VO. Use one when it carries *rules* or when parameter confusion creates real risk (money, identifiers, seat numbers). A `Name` that merely wraps a `String` without enforcing anything is noise.\n- Money is [[g:always]] a VO and [[r:never]] a `double` (09-distributed-tx-fintech.correctness-where-money-is-involved.q4) — the canonical example, and one that is universally accepted.\n:::","difficulty":"core"},{"id":"24-domain-driven-design.tactical-modelling-it.q2","q":"Aggregates: where should the boundary lie, and how large should an aggregate be?","a":"An [[b:aggregate]] is a cluster of objects that are loaded, saved, and [[g:kept consistent]] together. The [[b:aggregate root]] is the sole entry point into that cluster.\n\n<figure class='dgm'><svg viewBox='0 0 680 260' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' font-family='IBM Plex Mono, monospace' font-size='10' text-anchor='middle'>\n<defs><marker id='dd2' markerWidth='9' markerHeight='9' refX='6' refY='3' orient='auto'><path d='M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 z' fill='#AE4536'/></marker></defs>\n<text x='340' y='18' fill='#14233A' font-weight='600' font-size='11.5'>Aggregate: one consistency boundary, one root</text>\n<rect x='30' y='34' width='280' height='150' rx='10' fill='#E4F1EB' stroke='#0B6E4F' stroke-width='2'/>\n<text x='170' y='52' fill='#085239' font-weight='600'>Aggregate: Booking</text>\n<rect x='56' y='62' width='228' height='34' rx='6' fill='#FFFFFF' stroke='#0B6E4F' stroke-width='1.5'/>\n<text x='170' y='76' fill='#085239' font-weight='600'>Booking  ← AGGREGATE ROOT</text><text x='170' y='90' fill='#42536C'>enforces invariants for the cluster</text>\n<rect x='56' y='104' width='108' height='30' rx='6' fill='#FFFFFF' stroke='#7E8DA3'/><text x='110' y='123' fill='#42536C'>Passenger</text>\n<rect x='176' y='104' width='108' height='30' rx='6' fill='#FFFFFF' stroke='#7E8DA3'/><text x='230' y='123' fill='#42536C'>SeatHold</text>\n<rect x='56' y='142' width='228' height='30' rx='6' fill='#FFFFFF' stroke='#7E8DA3'/><text x='170' y='161' fill='#42536C'>Money (value object)</text>\n<text x='170' y='200' fill='#0B6E4F' font-weight='600'>Load / save / lock as ONE UNIT</text>\n<text x='170' y='216' fill='#42536C'>one transaction = one aggregate</text>\n<rect x='400' y='60' width='250' height='96' rx='10' fill='#F7F9FC' stroke='#7E8DA3' stroke-dasharray='4 3'/>\n<text x='525' y='80' fill='#42536C' font-weight='600'>Aggregate: Flight</text>\n<text x='525' y='100' fill='#42536C'>root: Flight</text>\n<text x='525' y='118' fill='#7E8DA3'>a DIFFERENT aggregate → do not</text><text x='525' y='132' fill='#7E8DA3'>retain an object reference</text>\n<line x1='312' y1='108' x2='396' y2='108' stroke='#AE4536' stroke-width='1.6' marker-end='url(#dd2)'/>\n<text x='354' y='100' fill='#AE4536' font-size='9' font-weight='600'>retain only</text><text x='354' y='124' fill='#AE4536' font-size='9'>FlightId</text>\n<rect x='30' y='228' width='620' height='24' rx='6' fill='#F6E8E5' stroke='#e6c6c0'/>\n<text x='340' y='244' fill='#7d2f24'>Large aggregate = broad locks, high contention. Small aggregate = consistency via events.</text>\n</svg></figure>\n\n**Vaughn Vernon's four rules — know these and you can answer most aggregate questions:**\n\n1. **Protect invariants within one boundary.** Anything that must be true *immediately* belongs in the same aggregate.\n2. **Keep aggregates small.** Start small; enlarge one only when an invariant requires it.\n3. **Reference other aggregates by [[o:id]], not by object.** Use `booking.flightId`, not `booking.flight`.\n4. **One transaction = one aggregate.** If several aggregates must change, use [[g:eventual consistency]] through domain events (24-domain-driven-design.tactical-modelling-it.q3).\n\n<pre><code><span class=\"k\">public class</span> Booking {                       <span class=\"c\">// AGGREGATE ROOT</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">private final</span> BookingId id;\n    <span class=\"k\">private final</span> FlightId flightId;           <span class=\"c\">// rule 3: id only</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">private final</span> List&lt;Passenger&gt; passengers;   <span class=\"c\">// inside the boundary</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">private</span> BookingStatus status;\n<span class=\"c\">    </span>\n    <span class=\"k\">public void</span> <span class=\"f\">addPassenger</span>(Passenger p) {      <span class=\"c\">// every change goes through the ROOT</span>\n        <span class=\"k\">if</span> (status != DRAFT) <span class=\"k\">throw new</span> DomainException(<span class=\"s\">\"booking is finalized; passengers cannot be added\"</span>);\n        <span class=\"k\">if</span> (passengers.<span class=\"f\">size</span>() &gt;= <span class=\"n\">9</span>) <span class=\"k\">throw new</span> DomainException(<span class=\"s\">\"maximum 9 passengers\"</span>);\n        passengers.<span class=\"f\">add</span>(p);                     <span class=\"c\">// the invariant is enforced HERE</span>\n    }\n    <span class=\"k\">public</span> List&lt;Passenger&gt; <span class=\"f\">passengers</span>() {\n        <span class=\"k\">return</span> List.<span class=\"f\">copyOf</span>(passengers);        <span class=\"c\">// prevents callers from mutating state by bypassing the root</span>\n    }\n}</code></pre>\n\n:::warn The sizing trap\nA [[r:large]] aggregate locks the whole cluster: two users modifying different parts still collide, and loading one booking may fetch 500 rows.\n:::\n\nAn aggregate that is [[r:too small]] leaves invariants spanning several aggregates, so events and compensation must maintain them. The deciding question is always: *which invariant absolutely must hold immediately?*\n\n:::deep\n- \"A seat must not be booked twice\" should [[o:not]] live in a `Flight` aggregate containing 300 seats. That would be a hot aggregate in which everyone booking the same flight contends.\n- A [[g:database constraint / atomic update]] (16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.presenting-dissecting-the-ota-project.q2) is a better fit. Recognizing this demonstrates where the theory's boundary lies.\n- References by id have secondary benefits: aggregates serialize compactly, do not pull in half the database, and later service extraction requires almost no domain-model changes.\n- For concurrency, an aggregate is the natural unit of [[g:optimistic locking]]: keep one `version` column on the root; when two users edit concurrently, the later writer retries (05-db-core-index-lock.transactions-mvcc-locking.q3).\n- Provide [[g:one repository per aggregate root]], not one per table. A `PassengerRepository` is a sign that `Passenger` has been pulled outside its boundary.\n- Aggregate boundaries [[o:do not]] correspond one-to-one with tables — one aggregate can span several tables, and the persistence adapter reassembles it (12-architecture-patterns.clean-layered-building-it-for-real.q2).\n- Start with [[g:small]] aggregates and merge them when you see an invariant being violated. The reverse direction — splitting a large aggregate — is much harder.\n:::","difficulty":"advanced"},{"id":"24-domain-driven-design.tactical-modelling-it.q3","q":"How do domain events differ from integration events, and where should each be published?","a":"The distinction is [[g:semantic and contractual]], not “in-process versus Kafka” or “inside versus outside a transaction.”\n\n<table class=\"cmp\"><thead><tr><th></th><th>Domain event</th><th>Integration event</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Meaning</td><td>a business fact meaningful inside a bounded context</td><td>a fact/notification intentionally exposed to another context</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Contract owner</td><td>the domain model/team; may evolve with the model</td><td>producer and consumers; compatibility and lifecycle are explicit</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Delivery</td><td>in-memory, persisted, or both</td><td>broker, webhook, stream, file, or another inter-context channel</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Payload</td><td>stable values needed by local handlers; avoid mutable aggregate references</td><td>consumer-oriented schema: notification-by-ID or event-carried state are deliberate trade-offs</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Reliability</td><td>depends on the local dispatch/storage design</td><td>outbox/relay, retries, idempotency, ordering, schema and observability contract</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nAn aggregate may record immutable domain events without knowing infrastructure. The application layer can handle local reactions and translate selected committed facts into versioned integration events.\n\nNot every domain event should leave the bounded context, and an external contract should not leak an internal aggregate object.\n\n<pre><code><span class=\"k\">@Transactional</span>\n<span class=\"k\">public</span> HoldResult <span class=\"f\">hold</span>(HoldSeatCommand cmd) {\n    Booking booking = bookings.<span class=\"f\">get</span>(cmd.bookingId());\n    booking.<span class=\"f\">hold</span>(cmd.seat());                  <span class=\"c\">// records SeatHeld domain fact</span>\n    bookings.<span class=\"f\">save</span>(booking);\n<span class=\"c\">    </span>\n    <span class=\"k\">for</span> (DomainEvent event : booking.<span class=\"f\">pullEvents</span>()) {\n        localHandlers.<span class=\"f\">handle</span>(event);             <span class=\"c\">// local policy; semantics must be explicit</span>\n        integrationMapper.<span class=\"f\">map</span>(event)\n            .<span class=\"f\">ifPresent</span>(outbox::<span class=\"f\">append</span>);       <span class=\"c\">// same DB transaction as aggregate</span>\n    }\n    <span class=\"k\">return</span> HoldResult.<span class=\"f\">ok</span>();\n}\n<span class=\"c\">// a separate relay publishes committed outbox rows and retries safely</span></code></pre>\n\n:::deep\nTransaction timing is an implementation choice. A local domain-event handler may run before commit because it contributes to the same invariant, after commit for a best-effort side effect, or asynchronously from durable storage. Document the failure/rollback semantics.\n\n`@TransactionalEventListener(AFTER_COMMIT)` avoids publishing before rollback but is not durable across a process crash.\n\nFor reliable external publication, write the integration event/outbox row atomically with the state change, then publish after commit through a relay (09-distributed-tx-fintech.distributed-transaction-patterns.q3).\n\nConsumers still need idempotency because commit/publish acknowledgement races create duplicates (08-message-queue.reliability-delivery-semantics.q6).\n\nPayload strategy is not “always IDs only.” Notification events minimize coupling and exposure but make consumers call back and depend on current producer state.\n\nEvent-carried state transfer improves consumer autonomy and historical meaning but duplicates data, increases privacy/schema obligations, and can grow messages.\n\nInclude the minimum stable facts required by the declared consumer contract, classify sensitive fields, and version compatibility deliberately.\n\nName facts in past tense (`SeatHeld`), include event ID, occurrence time, producer/schema version and domain identifiers where needed, and distinguish occurrence time from publish time.\n\nDomain events also do not imply event sourcing; that is a separate persistence decision (12-architecture-patterns.domain-driven-design.q4).\n:::","difficulty":"advanced"},{"id":"24-domain-driven-design.tactical-modelling-it.q4","q":"Repository, Factory, and Domain Service: where does each belong, and when is it needed?","a":"These three building blocks are often put in the wrong place.\n\n**Repository — a collection of aggregates, not a DAO.**\n\n<pre><code><span class=\"c\">// RIGHT: interface in domain/application, expressed in domain language</span>\n<span class=\"k\">public interface</span> BookingRepository {\n    Optional&lt;Booking&gt; <span class=\"f\">findById</span>(BookingId id);\n    List&lt;Booking&gt; <span class=\"f\">findExpiredHolds</span>(Instant now);\n    <span class=\"k\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">save</span>(Booking booking);            <span class=\"c\">// saves the ENTIRE aggregate</span>\n}\n<span class=\"c\">// WRONG: a DAO masquerading as a repository</span>\n<span class=\"k\">interface</span> BookingRepository {\n    <span class=\"k\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">updateStatusById</span>(Long id, <span class=\"k\">int</span> status);   <span class=\"c\">// bypasses the domain</span>\n    List&lt;Object[]&gt; <span class=\"f\">findRawJoin</span>();                  <span class=\"c\">// leaks persistence concerns</span>\n}</code></pre>\n\nProvide one repository for [[g:each aggregate root]] (24-domain-driven-design.tactical-modelling-it.q2), returning [[g:domain objects]] rather than JPA entities (12-architecture-patterns.clean-layered-building-it-for-real.q2).\n\n**Factory — when object creation is complex or must protect invariants at construction time.** If a constructor is sufficient, [[r:do not]] create a factory.\n\nUse one when construction draws from several sources, rules select a subtype, or a named operation better expresses intent (`Booking.draftFor(flightId, passengers)`).\n\n**Domain Service — only when behavior [[o:does not belong to]] any entity or VO.** Good signals are logic that needs *several* aggregates, or a pure stateless calculation.\n\n<pre><code><span class=\"c\">// Appropriate: pricing needs a fare table + fare class + promotions — it belongs to none alone</span>\n<span class=\"k\">public class</span> FareCalculator {\n    <span class=\"k\">public</span> Money <span class=\"f\">fareFor</span>(Flight f, FareClass c, List&lt;Promotion&gt; promos) { ... }\n}</code></pre>\n\n:::warn Domain Service is the most abused\nIf behavior [[o:uses only one aggregate's data]], it belongs on that aggregate. Pushing everything into `XxxDomainService` recreates an anemic domain (12-architecture-patterns.patterns-principles.q3) under a different name.\n:::\n\n:::deep\n**Distinguish the three kinds of service:**\n\n- An [[b:Application service]] represents a use case, coordinates work, and opens the transaction: (12-architecture-patterns.clean-layered-building-it-for-real.q3)\n- A [[b:Domain service]] contains pure business rules and knows nothing about transactions.\n- An [[b:Infrastructure service]] is an adapter that calls an external system.\n- They belong to different layers; do not merge them.\n- Repository methods should be [[g:driven by real use-case needs]]. `findAll()` over a table with tens of millions of rows is a trap; when paginating, use keyset pagination (18-query-optimization.rewriting-the-query-reshaping-the-model.q2).\n- Complex reporting queries should [[r:not]] be forced into an aggregate repository. They belong to a separate read model (12.8 CQRS), which may query a projection directly.\n- The Specification pattern wraps a business predicate in a reusable object (`new ExpiredHold(now)`), preventing a repository from accumulating dozens of `findByXxxAndYyy` methods.\n- Factory and Repository complement each other: a factory creates a [[o:new]] aggregate; a repository reconstructs an [[o:existing]] one. Both return aggregates in valid states.\n- At interview depth, saying *\"a repository is a domain concept; a JPA repository is an implementation detail\"* is enough to distinguish the model from habitual Spring Data usage.\n:::","difficulty":"extra"},{"id":"24-domain-driven-design.tactical-modelling-it.q5","q":"How do you turn a DDD model into an executable boundary and review checklist?","a":"A DDD model becomes useful when each important term is tied to an owner, invariant, command, persistence boundary and failure response. A context map or EventStorming board alone is not evidence that the boundary works.\n\n<table class=\"cmp\"><thead><tr><th>Worksheet field</th><th>Example question</th><th>Executable proof</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Language and owner</td><td>Does `hold` mean the same thing for Booking, Inventory and Payment? Who decides?</td><td>Glossary, named team owner, scenario review and unresolved-term log</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Invariant</td><td>What must be true immediately, and which aggregate protects it?</td><td>Domain/property/concurrency test plus DB constraint or version check where needed</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Transaction and authority</td><td>Which state is authoritative, and what is allowed to be eventually consistent?</td><td>Transaction boundary, outbox/relay or reconciliation design, freshness SLO</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Context relationship</td><td>Who supplies, consumes, translates or conforms to whose model?</td><td>Versioned contract, ACL mapping tests, compatibility window and ownership</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Failure and evolution</td><td>What if a command, event, projection, consumer or model migration is retried or delayed?</td><td>Idempotency/replay test, recovery runbook, schema migration and observability signals</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n:::deep\n- Draw a boundary around a semantic and consistency problem, not around nouns or tables. If two teams use the same word differently, that is evidence for a context boundary; if they share one invariant, it may be evidence for one aggregate or an explicit coordination workflow.\n- Separate domain events from integration events. A local event may evolve with the model; an external event needs a consumer contract, compatibility and privacy policy. Publish durable external facts after the source transaction through the canonical Outbox/Saga owner in Topic 09.\n- Add a negative case to every model: stale command, duplicate event, unknown legacy code, projection lag, delete/redaction request, and a boundary-crossing transaction. A model that only describes the happy path is not ready.\n- Review the model with production signals: conflict rate, aggregate load/lock duration, event lag, reconciliation age, translation failures, change-impact distance and number of coordinated deploys.\n- Keep the model proportional. A low-risk CRUD context may need a small vocabulary and explicit API contract; a payment or booking context needs stronger invariants, audit/recovery and domain-expert sign-off.\n:::","difficulty":"advanced"}]}]},"hasVi":true,"stem":"24-domain-driven-design","sourceOwners":{"10-system-design-rate-limit.framework-building-blocks.q1":"design-review-framework","10-system-design-rate-limit.framework-building-blocks.q2":"design-review-framework","10-system-design-rate-limit.framework-building-blocks.q7":"design-review-framework","11-system-design-cases.the-big-prompts.q12":"design-review-framework","11-system-design-cases.the-big-prompts.q21":"design-review-framework","10-system-design-rate-limit.framework-building-blocks.q3":"traffic-caching-building-blocks","10-system-design-rate-limit.framework-building-blocks.q4":"traffic-caching-building-blocks","10-system-design-rate-limit.framework-building-blocks.q5":"traffic-caching-building-blocks","10-system-design-rate-limit.framework-building-blocks.q6":"traffic-caching-building-blocks","10-system-design-rate-limit.surviving-high-load.q1":"surviving-high-load","10-system-design-rate-limit.surviving-high-load.q2":"surviving-high-load","10-system-design-rate-limit.surviving-high-load.q3":"surviving-high-load","10-system-design-rate-limit.surviving-high-load.q4":"surviving-high-load","10-system-design-rate-limit.surviving-high-load.q5":"surviving-high-load","10-system-design-rate-limit.rate-limiting-in-depth.q1":"distributed-rate-limiter","10-system-design-rate-limit.rate-limiting-in-depth.q2":"distributed-rate-limiter","10-system-des